# Approachable interface — anyone can DM

D&D Easy serves imaginative people, **not** technical people. Assume the user has never heard of Markdown, APIs, JSON, or localStorage. Every screen must be usable by someone whose only qualification is wanting to run a game.

## Core principle

- **Design for general-population technical aptitude.** If a control needs prior software knowledge to understand, rewrite it.
- **Fantasy theme is a usability tool, not wallpaper.** Use familiar tabletop metaphors to *explain* function: the Library is a bookshelf, Creation Files (CFs) are your saved prep notes, the Virtual Table is the game table. A themed label must make the feature *easier* to guess, never harder.

## Language rules

| Avoid (jargon) | Prefer (plain + themed) |
|----------------|-------------------------|
| "Markdown output" | "table-ready pages you can print or copy" |
| "Seed" (unexplained) | "Creation File (CF) — story notes the generators build from" |
| "Sync folder / localStorage" | "your auto-save folder — a folder on your computer" |
| "SRD 5.2.1 catalogue" | "the free official rules included with the app" |
| "API key missing" | "AI features are off — everything else still works" |

- First mention of a term of art on any screen gets a plain-language gloss.
- Error messages say what happened and what to do next, in one sentence each. Never surface raw error codes alone.

## Layout & affordance rules

- Every primary action answers "what will happen when I click this?" from its label alone — add a one-line subtitle if the label can't.
- Empty states teach: say what belongs here and give the first-step button (see `dm-control-tower` — manual path first).
- Icons are always paired with text; never icon-only for primary navigation.
- One obvious next step per screen. Progressive disclosure ("Where is my data?"-style expanders) over walls of settings.

## When theming, check

1. Does the metaphor match a real tabletop object or ritual DMs know? (bookshelf, character sheet, battle map, handout)
2. Would a first-time user guess the function from the themed label alone? If not, keep the theme but add the plain word.
3. Does decoration (dividers, badges, icons) mark *structure* — sections, tiers, states — or is it noise?

Related principles: `dm-control-tower` (manual-first), `workshop-library` (provenance labels use canonical plain terms).
